WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:18.666
Today I'd like to tell you how I became a scientist.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:22.332
I decided I wanted to be a scientist when I was 9 years old.

00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:26.266
Actually, I think that I didn't decide that I wanted to be a scientist.

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:30.632
I decided that I wanted to continue to explore things,

00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:33.332
take them apart, see if I could put them back together.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:40.032
Part of the reason I became a scientist was because of something that my uncle said to me.

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:43.399
He had a Master's degree in Chemistry, and he showed me one day

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.199
a paper that he had written that had just appeared in the Journal of Chemistry.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.299
It had some title that made absolutely no sense to me.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.466
It was long, and it had these figures in it, and he took the paper, and he said to me,

00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.199
"This was easier to write than an English paper."

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.699
That made a big impression on me, because papers were not easy to write.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.799
So, that was intriguing. I think the other thing that got me started

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.732
was that my mother had told me that she wanted to be a botanist.

00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:15.399
She was very interested in plants and how they worked.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.399
But, as a child, she had gotten rheumatic fever,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.332
missed a year of class, was promoted an extra year ahead,

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:26.066
and she kind of never caught up with arithmetic and mathematics.

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:31.766
To her credit, when I was 13, my mother went back to school, took a class in statistics,

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:37.832
which was very difficult, but she did pass the course, and that taught me something about the

00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:43.199
importance of persistence and of not being afraid to do the things that you want to do.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.899
When I got to college... Actually, it was when I was in junior high school,

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.566
I went to a program that the National Science Foundation had.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.866
It was called Summer Science Training Program.

00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:59.266
And kids in junior high school got together at a college campus,

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:03.866
and we got to do experiments and listen to lectures. It was a wonderful summer.

00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:07.199
It was in a small campus in Tyler, Texas,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.799
The campus organizers bought me the book, Calciphylaxis.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.299
I had read a Reader's Digest article about Hans Selya,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.099
and was interested in the stress response in animals and humans.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.566
So, I bought his textbook, which I mostly did not understand.

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:26.366
I don't remember much about the experiments,

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:29.532
except that I did take out adrenal glands and followed the rats.

00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:32.066
I took the rats home with me, by the way.

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:33.966
Many of them got away and there were...

00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:37.566
For many years, you could find black and white rats in the neighborhood now and then.

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:43.432
So, that kind of solidified my interest in what a wonderful thing science might be.

00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:47.966
It was really fun. It was a group effort that we had in Texas.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:52.932
I made very good friends there, and I felt very satisfied that I was doing something

00:02:52.933 --> 00:02:55.532
with both my brains and my hands.

00:02:55.533 --> 00:02:57.199
That was very satisfying.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.966
So, when I went to college, I wrote to someone at the University of Washington,

00:03:01.967 --> 00:03:06.866
where I was thinking of going and asked, "How do you become a physiologist?"

00:03:06.867 --> 00:03:08.599
which is what Hans Selya was.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:14.032
And he told me that as an undergraduate, I should go take... I should major in chemistry.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:17.032
I went off to the University of Washington. It's the only school I applied to,

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:20.499
and I applied there because I had an uncle in Seattle.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:26.266
Well, chemistry was pretty hard. And, although I'd been a very good student in high school,

00:03:26.267 --> 00:03:29.932
I found myself struggling, and so about mid-way through the year,

00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:35.332
I went to my advisor, whose name I can't remember for the life of me.

00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:37.832
I'd like to remember it, because I'd like to embarrass him.

00:03:37.833 --> 00:03:41.266
And I told him I was having difficult in introductory chemistry,

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:46.032
and he said, "Well, of course you're having trouble. Chemistry's not for girls."

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:49.532
Being a freshman, and not knowing any better, I said,

00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:52.299
"Oh, ok." And I went and changed my major.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.866
I went through 5 majors in my first 2 years as an undergraduate.

00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:02.366
I went from chemistry, I went completely outside of science into history.

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:07.032
Didn't like that because the first class was really big and pretty boring, actually.

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:11.966
Then I went and found the history of medicine, which was extremely interesting.

00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:17.899
And I found then, there, that library work -- looking into magazines and history --

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.266
could be another exciting way of exploring things.

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:24.132
And I almost did that, but there was really only one person

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.299
who was doing that at the University of Washington.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.866
And, you know, you're really sitting by yourself in the library most of the time.

00:04:29.867 --> 00:04:35.199
I then went back to biology, and had the very good fortune to take a class

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.832
that was given by Robert Kohn.

00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:39.366
It was the only time the class was given because

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:43.766
it was very expensive and unwieldy to give for undergraduates.

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:51.132
In the class, we used radioisotopes, we were in the lab 24 hours a day, taking turns in sleeping bags.

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.266
Following experiments as we went.

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:56.999
We also had visitors -- visiting scientists -- who came in

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.232
and treated us as serious people who wanted to listen to their results.

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:06.266
One of them was John Gurdon. That was a surprise to me, because we had read Gurdon's papers,

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:09.299
and they were very important papers in the field.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.299
Though I had envisioned him as an older man with grey hair and glasses

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.666
because that's what a scientist looks like, right?

00:05:15.667 --> 00:05:21.032
Of course, I was wrong. I was impressed with how young he was, this red-headed man

00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:26.432
full of energy and fun who obviously delighted in telling us about the experiments he had done

00:05:26.433 --> 00:05:29.332
and the way in which he had come to his conclusions.

00:05:29.333 --> 00:05:33.566
That kind of solidified my interest in developmental biology.

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:39.432
That question of how, from a single cell, you end up nine months later with a human being,

00:05:39.433 --> 00:05:44.232
who grows up. Truly, a remarkable, miraculous series of events.

00:05:44.233 --> 00:05:48.299
And how that exactly happens is, of course, one of the most interesting questions

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.432
that still stands before us.

00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:53.299
We've learned a lot, but we don't really have the whole picture yet.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.999
So, I decided to do developmental biology,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.499
I asked my professors how I should go about this, and they told me I had to go to graduate school.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.666
So, I said, "Ok." Now, there was one small problem

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:07.866
During those years when I was having trouble with chemistry,

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:12.599
I was also discovering the joys of a social life, and I was going out with a lot of people,

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.132
and I was skipping classes

00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:18.799
and so forth. So, my grades were, shall we say, a little erratic.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.799
Well, about that time, I met a medical student,

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:25.866
or really what happened was he tripped me in the cafeteria in Seattle.

00:06:25.867 --> 00:06:29.999
We went out -- he had to make up for tripping me, after all.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.932
And, we got together and it looked like that was something that might last.

00:06:33.933 --> 00:06:37.432
But then, he was going to go off to do his residency on the East Coast.

00:06:37.433 --> 00:06:42.132
So, I asked my advisors about schools on the East Coast, where he would be,

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:47.366
and since he was going to be in Bethesda, they said why don't you go to Johns Hopkins?

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:51.932
I couldn't go to Johns Hopkins because that was an all-men's school at the time.

00:06:51.933 --> 00:06:52.866
Dates me a bit.

00:06:52.867 --> 00:06:59.599
So, I ended up at Goucher college, which was close by and the sister college - an all woman's college.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.332
A very good college for me because it was very small,

00:07:02.333 --> 00:07:07.132
very different from the University of Washington, and I got a lot of attention from the professors,

00:07:07.133 --> 00:07:13.266
including one of them found me a job for the summer at NIH in the laboratory of Loretta Levi,

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:18.866
who was a microbiologist and who introduced me to the concept of molecular biology.

00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:23.299
She pretty much taught me everything from how to hold the pipette to the fact that

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:28.932
you really, really have to have small stretches of dialysis membrane

00:07:28.933 --> 00:07:32.599
if you don't want them to get all tangled up in the stir bar in the flask.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:39.566
She was the person who would be writing references for me when I was applying to graduate school.

00:07:39.567 --> 00:07:45.432
I applied in Boston because that's where my then-husband was going to be as a doctor.

00:07:45.433 --> 00:07:50.666
Well, I applied to every school in Boston except MIT.

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:53.932
Loretta asked me why I hadn't applied to MIT, and I said,

00:07:53.933 --> 00:07:57.299
"I hadn't applied to MIT because my vision of MIT was a bunch of guys

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.266
"running around with slide-rules doing math."

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:04.099
And I was not fond of math, and I wasn't very good at it either.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.032
So, she looked at me, and she said,

00:08:06.033 --> 00:08:10.032
"You have told me that you want to do developmental biology and molecular biology.

00:08:10.033 --> 00:08:13.199
"If you want to do that, you have to apply to MIT,

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.466
"because that's the best program in the country right now."

00:08:15.467 --> 00:08:17.532
So, I applied to MIT.

00:08:17.533 --> 00:08:22.866
And, it's a good thing that I did, because MIT is the only school in the Boston area that accepted me.

00:08:22.867 --> 00:08:27.966
That was an important lesson. You will not get anything that you do not ask for.

00:08:27.967 --> 00:08:30.499
You will not get anything that you don't apply for.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.666
So, do it. Just apply for it.

00:08:33.667 --> 00:08:38.132
Getting into MIT was one of the best things that ever happened to me.

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:42.332
It was an extremely exciting place to be a graduate student or a scientist.

00:08:42.333 --> 00:08:44.732
MIT is really graduate-student heaven.

00:08:44.733 --> 00:08:47.766
The science that was going on was remarkable.

00:08:47.767 --> 00:08:50.666
The faculty considered us their junior partners.

00:08:50.667 --> 00:08:55.299
We were talking all the time about something interesting going on and learning all the time.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.666
I worked there with David Baltimore and Harvey Lodish

00:08:58.667 --> 00:09:04.132
who had very different, but complementary approaches to science and who taught me a lot.

00:09:04.133 --> 00:09:09.099
Developmental biology wasn't... there wasn't somebody in that department

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:11.832
that I wanted to work with who was doing developmental biology.

00:09:11.833 --> 00:09:15.832
So, instead, I did a joint project with David Baltimore and Harvey Lodish

00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:20.299
that used virology that David was doing and protein synthesis

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.799
-- cell-free protein synthesis -- that Harvey was doing,

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.466
and I put them together. I was the first student at MIT to have joint PhD advisors,

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:33.566
and it was an interesting way to do it and very useful.

00:09:33.567 --> 00:09:36.766
Except for the part when I started writing papers.

00:09:36.767 --> 00:09:41.632
For a while, I would take my paper to one of them and they would make suggestions on it,

00:09:41.633 --> 00:09:45.832
and then I would take it to the other, and they would reverse the suggestions.

00:09:45.833 --> 00:09:51.799
So, I finally took to just going directly between the two of them before we got the first paper out.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:58.766
David and Harvey were extremely good mentors for me for different reasons.

00:09:58.767 --> 00:10:05.032
They expected a lot of their students. It was there that I learned that hard work can be extremely fun,

00:10:05.033 --> 00:10:08.332
especially if you're working with a group of other people.

00:10:08.333 --> 00:10:11.066
We worked hard and we played hard.

00:10:11.067 --> 00:10:12.599
And, we learned a lot.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:16.532
And those people are still very good friends and close colleagues today.

00:10:16.533 --> 00:10:21.632
After graduate school, the question was, "What do you do next?"

00:10:21.633 --> 00:10:24.666
And I wanted to go back to developmental biology.

00:10:24.667 --> 00:10:27.532
So, I began with listening to seminars.

00:10:27.533 --> 00:10:32.499
I heard Fotis Kafatos give a talk on the silk moth as a model system

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.932
for studying protein synthesis in a developing system.

00:10:35.933 --> 00:10:38.599
So, I went to talk to him, arranged a post-doc,

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.666
and I went back and told David and Harvey that I had my post-doc all set up.

00:10:42.667 --> 00:10:46.699
To my surprise, they were both very offended.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.099
And it turned out that they were offended because each of them thought

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:54.466
that I had gone to the other for advice and help in setting up the post-doc.

00:10:54.467 --> 00:10:58.032
And I learned then that part of the job of a good scientist is

00:10:58.033 --> 00:11:02.999
to mentor the scientists that come after them, the people they are training.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.166
And they took that very seriously.

00:11:05.167 --> 00:11:08.466
I also found out later, as I interacted with other people,

00:11:08.467 --> 00:11:11.099
that often the busiest scientists are the best mentors.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:15.332
They're the ones that are working the hardest, they're the ones that have the most connections,

00:11:15.333 --> 00:11:18.466
and they're the ones that it is worthwhile being persistent

00:11:18.467 --> 00:11:21.032
about getting to, and talking to, and learning from.

00:11:21.033 --> 00:11:27.532
I learned a lot in Fotis's lab, including the power and the value and the pain of failure.

00:11:27.533 --> 00:11:30.032
Things had worked very well as a graduate student.

00:11:30.033 --> 00:11:34.832
I got 6 papers out, had a lot of experiments, did a lot of collaborations

00:11:34.833 --> 00:11:37.799
with people not only at MIT, but around the world.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.932
So, when I got to Fotis's lab, and things didn't work, that was pretty tough to take.

00:11:41.933 --> 00:11:47.799
In addition, that was the time when the recombinant DNA controversy was all in full bloom.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.999
And for those of you who might not know,

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.132
that was the time at which there was a fear that if you took DNA from one source

00:11:54.133 --> 00:11:56.166
and connected it to DNA from another source,

00:11:56.167 --> 00:12:00.232
and then put it into a bacterium or a yeast or anything like that,

00:12:00.233 --> 00:12:05.099
you might create a new creature that would be a super dangerous germ or somesuch.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.566
Never very credible scientifically, but it had a lot of force socially.

00:12:09.567 --> 00:12:13.899
And what that meant for me in particular was that I could not do the science

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:15.299
that I wanted to do in Boston.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.999
It was banned in Boston and Cambridge.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.699
And so, I went to Cold Spring Harbor with Tom Maniatis,

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.399
and with a couple of his graduate students and post-docs,

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.299
and I spent the summer in Cold Spring Harbor, trying to do something,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.132
which in retrospect was not possible,

00:12:32.133 --> 00:12:36.599
which is to clone a very large genome into a plasmid, which is a small piece of DNA.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:42.699
I like to think that maybe that failure encouraged Maniatis

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:46.599
to think about the phage packaging system, which he developed shortly thereafter.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:51.699
I returned to Harvard with no positive results from a year at Cold Spring Harbor.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:58.099
That was very discouraging. At that point, a friend of mine, Argiris Efstratiadis who was in

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:04.066
Fotis's lab came to me and said, I'd like you to work on this project with me and Wally Gilbert.

00:13:04.067 --> 00:13:05.599
We're trying to clone insulin.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:09.466
Actually, what he said is, "We're trying to clone insulin."

00:13:09.467 --> 00:13:14.732
Arg was quite a character. He had been a Shakesperean actor in Greece,

00:13:14.733 --> 00:13:19.032
had a medical degree from Greece, but when he came to the US,

00:13:19.033 --> 00:13:24.332
he decided that his, as he said, "MD from Greece is useless."

00:13:24.333 --> 00:13:29.766
So, he entered the PhD program and was working on his graduate degree with Fotis.

00:13:29.767 --> 00:13:35.632
That project led to probably the most interesting project of my scientific career.

00:13:35.633 --> 00:13:39.099
First of all, everything worked. That practically never happens in science.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:43.299
It was six months from the beginning of the project until the publication of that paper.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.466
That paper and many others that were published during that time,

00:13:47.467 --> 00:13:50.599
led to a new direction for many biologists.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.966
It led biologists from the bench directly into the real world

00:13:53.967 --> 00:13:59.199
of business and applications of their science -- something which had not happened before.

00:13:59.200 --> 01:00:00.000
But that's another story that perhaps we'll approach another time.

